1)sudo apt update

Enter your password when requested. Wait for it to finish.
Then install Java and SSH:
2)sudo apt install openjdk-11-jdk ssh -y


Check Java:
3)java -version


Check SSH:
4)ssh -V


Both commands should display version information.
Step 3: Download Hadoop 3.3.6
Go to your home directory:
5)pwd
6)cd /home/osboxes


Download Hadoop:
7)wget https://archive.apache.org/dist/hadoop/common/hadoop-3.3.6/hadoop-3.3.6.tar.gz


Wait for the download to finish. Extract the file:
8)tar -xzf hadoop-3.3.6.tar.gz


Rename the extracted folder:
9)mv hadoop-3.3.6 hadoop


Check that it exists:
10)ls


You should see a folder named hadoop.
Check the Hadoop path:
11)cd /home/osboxes/hadoop
12)pwd


The expected path is:
/home/osboxes/hadoop


Step 4: Find the Java path
Run the command given in your manual:
13)dirname $(dirname $(readlink -f $(which java)))


For Java 11 in OSBoxes, the result will commonly be:
/usr/lib/jvm/java-11-openjdk-amd64


If your terminal prints a different path, use the path it actually displays.
Step 5: Configure hadoop-env.sh
Open the file:
14)nano /home/osboxes/hadoop/etc/hadoop/hadoop-env.sh


Find the JAVA_HOME line. Set it to:
export JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-11-openjdk-amd64


If the line begins with #, remove the #.
Save the file in Nano:
1. Press Ctrl + O.
2. Press Enter.
3. Press Ctrl + X.
Step 6: Configure Hadoop environment variables
Open the Bash configuration file:
nano ~/.bashrc


Go to the bottom and add:
export JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-11-openjdk-amd64
export HADOOP_HOME=/home/osboxes/hadoop
export HADOOP_INSTALL=$HADOOP_HOME
export HADOOP_MAPRED_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export HADOOP_COMMON_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export HADOOP_HDFS_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export HADOOP_YARN_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export HADOOP_COMMON_LIB_NATIVE=$HADOOP_HOME/lib/native
export PATH=$PATH:$HADOOP_HOME/bin:$HADOOP_HOME/sbin
export HADOOP_OPTS="-Djava.library.path=$HADOOP_HOME/lib/native"


Save and exit using Ctrl + O → Enter → Ctrl + X.
Apply the settings:
source ~/.bashrc


Verify the paths:
echo $JAVA_HOME
echo $HADOOP_HOME
hadoop version


The Hadoop version command should display information for Hadoop 3.3.6.
Step 7: Configure core-site.xml
Open the file:
nano /home/osboxes/hadoop/etc/hadoop/core-site.xml


Inside the existing <configuration> tags, add:
<property>
    <name>fs.defaultFS</name>
    <value>hdfs://localhost:9000</value>
</property>


The file should have this overall structure:
<configuration>
    <property>
        <name>fs.defaultFS</name>
        <value>hdfs://localhost:9000</value>
    </property>
</configuration>


Save and exit.
Step 8: Configure hdfs-site.xml
Open:
nano /home/osboxes/hadoop/etc/hadoop/hdfs-site.xml


For a single OSBoxes virtual machine, use the following configuration. The replication factor is set to 1 because only one DataNode is being configured; the manual's value of 3 is intended for a setup with multiple DataNodes.
<configuration>
    <property>
        <name>dfs.replication</name>
        <value>1</value>
    </property>
    <property>
        <name>dfs.namenode.name.dir</name>
        <value>file:///home/osboxes/hadoop/data/namenode</value>
    </property>
    <property>
        <name>dfs.datanode.data.dir</name>
        <value>file:///home/osboxes/hadoop/data/datanode</value>
    </property>
</configuration>


Save and exit.
Create the directories:
mkdir -p /home/osboxes/hadoop/data/namenode
mkdir -p /home/osboxes/hadoop/data/datanode


Step 9: Configure MapReduce
Open:
nano /home/osboxes/hadoop/etc/hadoop/mapred-site.xml


If the file does not exist, create it with the command above. Add:
<configuration>
    <property>
        <name>mapreduce.framework.name</name>
        <value>yarn</value>
    </property>
</configuration>


Save and exit.
Step 10: Configure YARN
Open:
nano /home/osboxes/hadoop/etc/hadoop/yarn-site.xml


Add the following inside <configuration>:
<property>
    <name>yarn.nodemanager.aux-services</name>
    <value>mapreduce_shuffle</value>
</property>
<property>
    <name>yarn.nodemanager.auxservices.mapreduce.shuffle.class</name>
    <value>org.apache.hadoop.mapred.ShuffleHandler</value>
</property>


Keep the opening and closing <configuration> tags around these properties. Save and exit.
Step 11: Configure SSH passwordless login
Hadoop uses SSH to start its services, even when running on a single computer.
Install SSH if necessary:
sudo apt install openssh-server -y


Start the SSH service:
sudo systemctl enable --now ssh


Generate an SSH key:
ssh-keygen -t rsa


Press Enter for each prompt to accept the default file location and an empty passphrase.
Add the public key to the authorized keys file:
cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys


Set the permissions:
chmod 700 ~/.ssh
chmod 640 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys


Test the connection:
ssh localhost


If asked whether to continue connecting, type yes. If it logs you in, type:
exit


Step 12: Format the NameNode
Do this before starting Hadoop for the first time.
hdfs namenode -format


Wait until the command finishes successfully. You should see a message indicating that the NameNode storage directory was formatted.
Important: Do not format the NameNode every time you start Hadoop. Formatting again can erase the filesystem metadata.
Step 13: Start Hadoop HDFS
Start the HDFS services:
start-dfs.sh


If prompted, enter your Ubuntu password only if SSH requests it.
Check the running Java processes:
jps


You should normally see processes similar to:
- NameNode
- DataNode
- SecondaryNameNode
- Jps
If these appear, HDFS is running.
Step 14: Test Hadoop with a file
Create a directory in HDFS:
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /user/osboxes


Create a local sample file:
echo "Hello Hadoop" > sample.txt


Upload the file to HDFS:
hdfs dfs -put sample.txt /user/osboxes/


List the HDFS directory:
hdfs dfs -ls /user/osboxes


Display the file's contents:
hdfs dfs -cat /user/osboxes/sample.txt


Expected output:
Hello Hadoop


This confirms that you can upload and read a file through HDFS.
Step 15: Stop Hadoop before shutting down
When you finish the experiment, run:
stop-dfs.sh


If you later start YARN for a MapReduce experiment, stop it as well:
stop-yarn.sh